     5	"""A 6T SRAM bit cell for IHP SG13G2 drawn at the standard (non-SRAM) rules, so that IHP's DRC
     6	deck passes it without the SRAM marker layer. Topology as in the PDK's own bit cell (a "thin"
     7	cell, point-symmetric about its centre):
     8	
     9	  x:  | NMOS strip L | well gap | PMOS L  PMOS R | well gap | NMOS strip R |
    10	      bit lines and VDD/VSS in Metal2 (vertical), word line in Metal3 (horizontal)
    11	
    12	Right half (the left half is the same rotated by 180 degrees):
    13	  NMOS strip, straight, W 0.30: BL contact (y = +YB, shared with the row above) | PG gate = WL |
    14	     Q_R contact (y = 0) | PD gate (input Q_L) | VSS contact (y = -YB, shared with the row below)
    15	  PMOS strip, W 0.15 with 0.30 contact pads (dogbone): VDD contact (y = -YB, shared) | PU gate
    16	     (input Q_L) | drain pad (Q_R) on the Q_R Metal1 track
    17	  The inverter gate poly jogs through a contacted pad in the well gap, which the Q_L Metal1
    18	  track reaches; the WL poly is contacted at the column boundary (shared with the mirrored
    19	  neighbour) and taken up to the Metal3 word line through a Metal2 island.
    20	
    21	The rules that set the pitch (versus the PDK cell, which uses relaxed rules under its SRAM
    22	marker): Cnt.c 0.07 Activ enclosure of Cont (PMOS pads 0.30 wide instead of 0.20), NW.c/NW.d
    23	0.31 (instead of 0.27 each), Gat.c 0.18 end cap. Y is set by Metal1 (BL pad, two storage-node
    24	tracks), as in the PDK cell.
    25	
    26	  uv run sram6t.py [COLS ROWS]   writes sram6t.gds with CELL6T, ARRAY6T (COLS x ROWS bits, rows
    27	                                  and columns mirrored, with a tap row at the top and bottom)
